    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "n = 2   ->  1    (1 + 0)\nn = 4   ->  3    (2 + 1)\nn = 10  ->  55"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The pattern: recursion basics"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The problems in this chapter are recursion in its plainest form: a base case, one or two smaller calls, and a way to combine them. Each one also shows what recursion **costs** — the extra calls of tree recursion, the stack memory of a long chain of calls — and when a loop or a smarter split does better."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Plain recursion"
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "fib-call-tree",
      "caption": "The call tree branches twice at every level, and the same small calls appear again and again."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function fib(n) {\n  if (n <= 1) return n;                  // base cases: fib(0) = 0, fib(1) = 1\n  return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);        // two calls: the call tree branches\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def fib(n):\n    if n <= 1:\n        return n                          # base cases: fib(0) = 0, fib(1) = 1\n    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)        # two calls: the call tree branches"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int fib(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return n;                 // base cases: fib(0) = 0, fib(1) = 1\n    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);       // two calls: the call tree branches\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int fib(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return n;                 // base cases: fib(0) = 0, fib(1) = 1\n    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);       // two calls: the call tree branches\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(2ⁿ)** (more precisely about 1.6ⁿ), **space O(n)** for the call stack. It's the textbook definition, but `fib(40)` already makes over 300 million calls."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. Recursion with memoization"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The call tree is full of repeats: `fib(n − 2)` is computed inside `fib(n − 1)` and again on its own. Store each answer the first time it's computed and return the stored value after that. Now each `fib(k)` is computed once."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function fib(n, memo = new Map()) {\n  if (n <= 1) return n;\n  if (memo.has(n)) return memo.get(n);   // computed before: reuse it\n  const value = fib(n - 1, memo) + fib(n - 2, memo);\n  memo.set(n, value);\n  return value;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "from functools import cache\n\n@cache                                    # remembers every fib(k) it has returned\ndef fib(n):\n    if n <= 1:\n        return n\n    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "Map<Integer, Integer> memo = new HashMap<>();\n\nint fib(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return n;\n    if (memo.containsKey(n)) return memo.get(n);   // computed before: reuse it\n    int value = fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);\n    memo.put(n, value);\n    return value;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "unordered_map<int, int> memo;\n\nint fib(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return n;\n    if (memo.count(n)) return memo[n];    // computed before: reuse it\n    int value = fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);\n    memo[n] = value;\n    return value;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n), space O(n)** for the memo and the call stack. This is **top-down dynamic programming** — the Dynamic Programming course starts exactly here."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "3. Iterate, keeping the last two"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function fib(n) {\n  let prev = 0, cur = 1;                 // F(0), F(1)\n  if (n === 0) return 0;\n  for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) [prev, cur] = [cur, prev + cur];   // slide one step forward\n  return cur;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def fib(n):\n    prev, cur = 0, 1                      # F(0), F(1)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    for _ in range(2, n + 1):\n        prev, cur = cur, prev + cur       # slide one step forward\n    return cur"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int fib(int n) {\n    if (n == 0) return 0;\n    int prev = 0, cur = 1;                // F(0), F(1)\n    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {\n        int next = prev + cur;            // slide one step forward\n        prev = cur;\n        cur = next;\n    }\n    return cur;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int fib(int n) {\n    if (n == 0) return 0;\n    int prev = 0, cur = 1;                // F(0), F(1)\n    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {\n        int next = prev + cur;            // slide one step forward\n        prev = cur;\n        cur = next;\n    }\n    return cur;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n), space O(1).** Each value needs only the two before it, so two variables are enough. (A 2 × 2 matrix power reaches O(log n), but it's rarely asked.)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
    },
    {
      "type": "complexity",
      "rows": [
        {
          "approach": "Plain recursion",
          "time": "O(2ⁿ)",
          "timeWhy": "Each call makes **two more**, recomputing the same values (about 1.6ⁿ calls).",
          "space": "O(n)",
          "spaceWhy": "The call stack goes **n** deep."
        },
        {
          "approach": "Recursion with memoization",
          "time": "O(n)",
          "timeWhy": "Each `fib(k)` is computed **once**, then read from the memo.",
          "space": "O(n)",
          "spaceWhy": "The memo and the call stack, **n** each."
        },
        {
          "approach": "Iterate, keeping the last two",
          "time": "O(n)",
          "timeWhy": "One loop of **n** additions.",
          "space": "O(1)",
          "spaceWhy": "Only the **last two values**."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Edge cases to test"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**n = 0 and n = 1** — the base cases themselves.",
        "**n = 2** — the first value built from both base cases.",
        "**Largest n** — F(30) = 832040 fits easily; beyond F(46) a 32-bit int overflows."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Variations"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Climbing Stairs** — the same recurrence in disguise (Dynamic Programming course).",
        "**N-th Tribonacci Number** — the sum of the last three.",
        "**Fibonacci modulo m** — the same loop, taking `% m` each step."
      ]
    }
  ],
